# Praxity Community Permission 1.0

Despite the PolyForm Perimeter Noncompete section, the licensor permits you to:

1. publicly distribute a source fork of the software without charge; or
2. provide the software as a Free Public Service.

This additional permission applies only while all of these conditions are met:

1. The fork or service visibly displays **“Praxity Accessibility Audit by Ariel Harlap”**
   linked to <https://github.com/Praxity/praxity-audit>. A fork must place it in
   its README or equivalent primary documentation; a service must place it
   somewhere users can readily find.
2. The software and its modifications are available as source code under these
   same terms.
3. A Free Public Service gives users a clear link to the complete corresponding
   source code for its deployed Praxity Accessibility Audit version and modifications,
   including the material needed to build and run those modifications. That
   source must remain available without charge while the service is offered.
   This condition does not extend to separate software that merely communicates
   with Praxity Accessibility Audit through documented command-line or file interfaces.
4. Reports generated or provided by a public source fork or Free Public Service
   preserve this attribution:

   > Generated by Praxity Accessibility Audit — created by Ariel Harlap
   >
   > <https://github.com/Praxity/praxity-audit>

**Free Public Service** means a publicly accessible service that charges no fee
or other consideration for access or results, is not white-labelled, is not
bundled with or gated by a paid offering, and is not operated primarily to
market or deliver a paid audit service. It does not display paid advertising,
require users to grant rights to their content or data beyond providing the
service, or sell faster processing, higher limits, extra features, or priority.
General donations to a nonprofit and general sponsorships, including a
reasonable sponsor acknowledgement, do not by themselves make a service paid
when they do not change access, results, or priority.

This permission does not cover paid access to Praxity Accessibility Audit or its reports,
paid audit services powered substantially by it, paid hosting, resale,
repackaging, rental, white-labelling, or providing it as substantial audit
functionality in a paid product. Those uses require separate written permission
from the licensor. Contact hello@arielharlap.com.

The required attribution grants permission to use the words “Praxity Accessibility Audit”
only for that attribution and to truthfully identify the software's origin. It
does not grant rights to Praxity logos or other marks, or permission to imply
endorsement or official status.
